 "old_string": "For the recurring task `task-dc578dca`, demos established a family of regular grids with outputs from 1x1 up to larger matrices. Two demos had singleton outputs matching a repeated same-color rectangular junction pattern. In one test, four color-6 junctions at the corners of a rectangular sublattice led to the accepted output `[[6]]`. This is useful evidence for that recurrence, not proof of a universal rule; see `references/task-dc578dca-case-notes.md` for prior concise evidence and rejected candidates. A separate accepted fresh instance (14×28) is documented in `references/task-dc578dca-2026-10-21-accepted-2x3.md`: exact output `[[1,8,4],[8,8,4]]`, following eight demos and five rejected submissions. Its competing output-size guesses were unreliable; the selector/size rule remains unresolved. Keep all recurrence branches separate and re-infer from the current demos; do not generalize the singleton rule or any output dimensions without evidence.",
 "new_string": "For the recurring task `task-dc578dca`, demos established a family of regular grids with outputs from 1x1 up to larger matrices. Two demos had singleton outputs matching a repeated same-color rectangular junction pattern. In one test, four color-6 junctions at the corners of a rectangular sublattice led to the accepted output `[[6]]`. This is useful evidence for that recurrence, not proof of a universal rule; see `references/task-dc578dca-case-notes.md` for prior concise evidence and rejected candidates. A separate accepted fresh instance (14×28) is documented in `references/task-dc578dca-2026-10-21-accepted-2x3.md`: exact output `[[1,8,4],[8,8,4]]`, following eight demos and five rejected submissions. Its competing output-size guesses were unreliable; the selector/size rule remains unresolved. Keep all recurrence branches separate and re-infer from the current demos; do not generalize the singleton rule or any output dimensions without evidence.\n\nA fresh accepted recurrence (2026-11-02) supports a more specific local rule: each output cell summarizes a 2x2 window of adjacent rail intersections, returning their common non-rail color when all four match, otherwise the panel fill. This exactly reproduces three demos and the accepted 6x4 test `[[4,3,6,6],[3,3,3,3],[6,3,3,3],[3,3,4,3],[4,3,3,3],[3,3,3,6]]`. The 2x2 mapping is supported, but selecting/cropping the junction lattice is still unresolved (one demo excludes apparent outer rails); see `references/task-dc578dca-2026-11-02-junction-window-accepted.md`. Do not infer universal output dimensions or rail selection from this branch."
}
